84:
In the phrase ‘by way of emptiness, singlefold, etc.’ (suññate-kavidhādīhi), first, the decision by way of emptiness (suññata) should be understood. Indeed, in the ultimate sense, all the truths should be understood as empty because of the absence of an experiencer, a doer, one who is extinguished, and a traveler. Therefore, it is said:

‘For there is suffering, but no sufferer is found;
The deed is there, but no doer is found;
Extinction there is, but no person extinguished;
The path exists, but no traveler is found.’

Alternatively—the first two truths (dukkha and samudaya) are empty of permanence, beauty, happiness, and self. The deathless state (nirodha) is empty of self. The path (magga) is devoid of permanence, happiness, and self. Thus, the decision by way of emptiness in those four truths should be understood. Or else—the three truths of dukkha, samudaya, and magga are empty of nirodha-sacca. Nirodha-sacca is also empty of the remaining three truths. Or else, among these truths, the causes (samudaya and magga) are empty of the effects because of the absence of the effects (dukkha and nirodha) in them. It is not like the ‘Prakṛti’ of the Proponents of Nature (Pakativādī), which is co-existent with its effect. The effects (dukkha and nirodha) are also empty of the causes because they cannot co-exist with the causes (samudaya and magga). It is not like the ‘double-atoms’ (dvi-aṇuka) etc. of the Atomists (Aṇuvādī), which co-exist with their causal atoms. Therefore, it is said:

‘Three are here empty of nirodha,
And extinction is empty of those three;
The cause is empty of the effect,
And the effect is empty of that cause.’

Thus, first, the decision ‘by way of emptiness’ should be understood.

According to the phrase ‘by way of singlefold, etc.’ (ekavidhādīhi)—among these four truths, the entire truth of suffering (dukkha-sacca) is onefold (ekavidha) as occurrence in saṃsāra; twofold (duvidha) as name and form; threefold (tividha) by way of the three types of becoming (kāma, rūpa, and arūpa); fourfold (catubbidha) by way of the four nutriments; and fivefold (pañcavidha) by way of the five aggregates of clinging. The truth of origin (samudaya-sacca) is also onefold as the producer of suffering; twofold as associated with wrong view and dissociated from wrong view; threefold by way of craving for sense-pleasures, craving for existence, and craving for non-existence; fourfold as that which is to be abandoned by the four paths; fivefold by way of delighting in the five aggregates (rūpābhinandana etc.); and sixfold by way of the six groups of craving. The truth of cessation (nirodha-sacca) is also onefold as the unconditioned element (asankhata-dhātu); in a figurative sense, it is twofold as cessation with residue and without residue; threefold as the quieting of the three types of becoming; fourfold as that which is to be attained by the four paths; fivefold as the quieting of the five types of delight; and sixfold as the destruction of the six groups of craving. The truth of the path (magga-sacca) is also onefold as that which is to be developed; twofold by way of serenity (samatha) and insight (vipassanā), or by way of seeing (dassana) and development (bhāvanā).

184:
...rebirth-consciousness, descending name-and-form, clarity as sense base, touching as contact, and experiencing as feeling. 'These five states in the future rebirth-existence occur due to the kamma performed in this present existence' (Paṭis. I. 47). Thus, this Wheel of Becoming has twenty modes as its spokes.

In that Wheel of Becoming, there are five kamma-accumulations in the past existence, five resultant-accumulations in the present existence, five kamma-accumulations in the present existence, and five resultant states in the future existence. Thus, ten states are called kamma, and ten states are called result (vipāka). In two places, kamma is called kamma; in two places, result is called result. Thus, this entire round of the structure of conditions, which is the Wheel of Becoming, is both kamma and the result of kamma (kamma-vipāka). Likewise, in two places, kamma is the 'summary of kamma' (kamma-saṅkhepa); in two places, result is the 'summary of result' (vipāka-saṅkhepa). Thus, this entire Wheel of Becoming is both the summary of kamma and the summary of result. In two places, kamma is the 'round of kamma' (kamma-vaṭṭa); in two places, result is the 'round of result' (vipāka-vaṭṭa). Thus, this entire Wheel of Becoming is both the round of kamma and the round of result. Likewise, in two places, kamma is 'kamma-becoming' (kamma-bhava); in two places, result is 'result-becoming' (vipāka-bhava). Thus, this entire Wheel of Becoming is both kamma-becoming and result-becoming. In two places, kamma is 'kamma-process' (kamma-puvatta); in two places, result is 'result-process' (vipāka-puvatta). Thus, this entire Wheel of Becoming is both kamma-process and result-process. Likewise, in two places, kamma is 'kamma-continuity' (kamma-santati); in two places, result is 'result-continuity' (vipāka-santati). Thus, this entire Wheel of Becoming is both kamma-continuity and result-continuity. In two places, kamma is called 'action' (kiriya); in two places, result is called 'the fruit of action' (kiriya-phala). Thus, this entire Wheel of Becoming is both action and the fruit of action.

Thus, this Wheel of Becoming, which arises well with its causes,
Is suffering, impermanent, unstable, transient, and not lasting.
States arise from states through appropriate causes;
Herein, there is no self nor another.
States produce states due to the support of the accumulation of causes;
For the cessation of causes, the Buddha has taught the Dhamma;
When causes are ceased, the broken round no longer revolves.
Thus, for the sake of making an end to the suffering of the round, the holy life exists in this Dispensation;
Since no being (self) is to be found, there is neither annihilationism nor eternalism.

In the phrase 'It revolves incessantly having three rounds,' formations and becoming are the 'round of kamma' (kamma-vaṭṭa); ignorance, craving, and clinging are the 'round of defilements' (kilesa-vaṭṭa); consciousness, name-and-form, the sixfold sense base, contact, and feeling are the 'round of results' (vipāka-vaṭṭa). Thus, this Wheel of Becoming has three revolutions through these three rounds. As long as the round of defilements is not cut off...

207:
## Commentary on the Instruction on Contemplation of the Body
'Internal' (ajjhatta) refers to one's own internal [state]. Therefore, 'ajjhattaṃ kāye' means 'in one's own body'. In that context, 'kāye' refers to the material body (rūpakāya). Here, the material body is intended as 'kāya' in the sense of a collection (samūha) of limbs, major and minor, and of things such as hair and so on, just like a collection of elephants, horses, or chariots.

Just as it is called 'kāya' in the sense of a collection, it is also called 'kāya' in the sense of being the place of origin (āya) of loathsome things. Because it is the place of origin of things that are extremely loathsome, that material body is also called 'kāya'. 'Āya' means the place of origin. The word-definition (vacanattha) here is: they arise (āyanti) from that, therefore it is 'āya'. What arise? Loathsome things such as hair and so on. Thus, because it is the place of origin of loathsome things such as hair and so on, it is called 'kāya'.

'Kāyānupassī' means one who has the habit of contemplating the body, or one who is contemplating the body. It should be understood that although 'kāye' (in the body) has already been said, the second mention of the word 'kāya' in 'kāyānupassī' is made for the purpose of showing the unmixed determination and the breaking down of compactness (ghana-vinibbhoga), and so on. By doing so, it is shown that one is not a contemplator of feelings, mind, or phenomena in the body, but rather a contemplator of the body in the body; thus, by seeing only the mode of body-contemplation in the object known as the body, unmixed determination is shown.

Similarly, one is not a contemplator of a single phenomenon separate from the major and minor limbs in the body, nor a contemplator of a woman or a man separate from hair, body hair, and so on. Even in this material body, which is a collection of primary elements (bhūtarūpa) and derived matter (upādārūpa) such as hair and body hair, one is not a contemplator of a single phenomenon separate from the primary elements and derived matter. Rather, like one looking at the components of a chariot, one contemplates the collection of major and minor limbs; like one looking at the parts of a city, one contemplates the collection of hair, body hair, and so on; and like one dissecting the layers of a banana trunk or one opening an empty fist, one is a contemplator of only the collection of primary elements and derived matter. Thus, by showing the object known as 'body' through its various parts and as a collection, the breaking down of compactness is shown.

In this material body, one does not find a 'body', a 'woman', a 'man', or any other thing separate from the aforementioned collection. However, it is only in this aforementioned collection of phenomena that beings wrongly cling in various ways. Therefore, the ancient teachers said:

\"What one sees, that is not seen;
What is seen, that one does not see;
Not seeing, the fool is bound;
Being bound, he is not released.\"
